import type { FaqItem } from '@components/MqttLearn/FaqAccordion.astro';

const faq: FaqItem[] = [
	{
		q: 'Is TBMQ a drop-in replacement for Mosquitto?',
		a: 'At the protocol level, yes — TBMQ speaks MQTT 3.1, 3.1.1 and 5.0, so your clients need no changes. Operationally, no: TBMQ runs with Kafka, Redis and PostgreSQL alongside it, which is a different deployment shape than a single binary.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I run TBMQ on a Raspberry Pi like Mosquitto?',
		a: "No — TBMQ targets server-class nodes in a cluster, not a constrained device. The usual pattern is a lightweight broker on the device or gateway bridging into a central TBMQ cluster, which is what Mosquitto's bridge and TBMQ's MQTT Integration are for.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Does Mosquitto support clustering?',
		a: 'No. Bridging forwards messages between brokers, but it shares no session or subscription state — so a bridged pair behaves as two brokers, not one cluster, and a session cannot resume on the other node.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How do I migrate from Mosquitto without downtime?',
		a: "Run both brokers with two bridge legs: Mosquitto's own bridge forwarding into TBMQ, and TBMQ's MQTT Integration forwarding back. Then repoint clients in batches and remove the bridges. No flag day and no maintenance window.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Is TBMQ free?',
		a: 'TBMQ Community Edition is open source under the Apache 2.0 license, including commercial use. Professional Edition adds single sign-on, role-based access control, audit logs and white labeling.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How many concurrent connections can Mosquitto handle?',
		a: 'There is no official figure — the Mosquitto project publishes no connection or throughput number. The ceiling depends on file descriptors, memory and the fact that message handling runs in one event loop on one host. <strong>The useful answer is that the limit is one machine</strong> — which is why the decision to move is usually about clustering and durability rather than a specific number.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I run TBMQ on a single node?',
		a: 'Yes, and it is how most people start. The standard Docker install runs one TBMQ node with Kafka, Redis and PostgreSQL beside it, which gives you the durability guarantee and the separate device and application paths without a cluster; you add nodes when you need the headroom or the availability. What a single node does not give you is a single binary — that is the trade this page is about.',
	},
];
---

<MqttTopicLayout slug="mosquitto-alternative" faq={faq}>
	<h2>What Mosquitto is built for</h2>
	<p>
		Mosquitto is a single binary of a few megabytes that runs on a router, a single-board computer or a spare VM, and it
		implements MQTT 5.0, shared subscriptions included. That combination is what makes it the usual choice for a first
		deployment — and it is a design that commits, deliberately, to one process on one host.
	</p>
	<p>
		That commitment is the whole story of this page. What Mosquitto does well follows from staying small, and what it
		cannot do follows from the same decision. So the question is not whether it is a good broker — it is whether your
		deployment has outgrown a single one.
	</p>
	<p>
		One scoping note: <strong>Mosquitto here means the open-source Eclipse project</strong>. Cedalo's commercial Pro
		Edition is a separate product with its own feature set and licence, and is not what the sections below compare.
	</p>

	<h2>The three walls</h2>
	<p>
		Deployments outgrow Mosquitto in three specific ways. They are worth naming precisely, because only the third one is
		really about scale.
	</p>

	<h3>The cluster wall</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto runs as a single broker. It can bridge to another broker, and a bridge does forward messages — but it
		shares no session state and no subscription state. Two bridged brokers are two brokers, not one logical broker: a
		client's session lives on the node it connected to, and if that node is gone, the session is gone with it. Vertical
		scaling is the only headroom you have, and one machine is the ceiling.
	</p>

	<h3>The durability wall</h3>
	<p>
		When Mosquitto returns a PUBACK, the message is in that process's memory, and it will reach disk at the next
		autosave of a single file on that single host. The acknowledgement your publisher receives is therefore a promise
		about one process on one machine.
	</p>

	<h3>The mixed-traffic wall</h3>
	<p>
		This is the one nobody markets against, and it is usually what actually hurts. IoT traffic is not one kind of
		traffic. Thousands of devices publish telemetry continuously. A handful of backend applications subscribe to
		high-volume streams and must not miss anything. Commands target one specific client and need low latency even when
		that client is briefly offline. Through a single delivery path those three contend for the same memory: one slow
		backend consumer applies backpressure that devices feel, and a burst of device publishing delays the command you
		just sent.
	</p>

	<h2>How TBMQ answers each one</h2>
	<p>
		TBMQ is built on <a href="https://kafka.apache.org/">Kafka</a> for durability and distribution,
		<a href="https://netty.io/">Netty</a> for non-blocking transport, an actor per client for concurrency, and an in-memory
		Trie for subscription matching. Those are not incidental choices — each one maps onto a wall above.
	</p>

	<h3>Acknowledged means stored</h3>
	<p>
		TBMQ does not send a PUBACK or PUBREC until Kafka has confirmed the write. Once the publisher holds that
		acknowledgement, the message no longer depends on the node that received it: if that node dies, another picks up
		from Kafka and continues delivery. Being precise about the limit — the shipped defaults keep a single copy (<code
			>acks=1</code
		>, replication factor <code>1</code>), so surviving the loss of a Kafka broker means running a replicated Kafka with
		matching producer acknowledgements. The guarantee is real; how far it extends is something you configure.
	</p>

	<h3>One cluster, no coordinator</h3>
	<p>
		Every TBMQ node is identical. There is no master, no leader election, and nothing that becomes a bottleneck or a
		single point of failure. Session and subscription state is shared through Kafka, so when a client reconnects after a
		node failure, any node in the cluster can resume its session. Starting a node is the entire scaling operation:
		consumer groups rebalance, load spreads across the larger cluster, and there is no resharding and no downtime.
	</p>
	<p>
		Identical nodes are also what keeps the deployment ordinary. TBMQ ships
		<a href="/docs/installation/cluster/helm-cluster-setup-aws/">Helm charts</a> for EKS, AKS, GKE and minikube, and every
		node exposes a <a href="/docs/other/health/">health endpoint</a> — so draining and replacing one is a Kubernetes rollout
		rather than a broker-specific procedure. No node has to go last.
	</p>

	<h3>Device and application traffic never share a path</h3>
	<p>
		TBMQ classifies persistent clients by how they actually behave. <strong>DEVICE</strong> clients publish often and subscribe
		to little; while one is offline its messages wait in a per-client queue in Redis or Valkey, which absorbs high write rates
		and hands the queue back quickly on reconnect. <strong>APPLICATION</strong> clients subscribe to high-volume topics, and
		each one gets a dedicated Kafka topic and its own consumer, so a backlog accumulates safely in Kafka and is replayed in
		order from that client's committed offset. A device publishing spike cannot delay an application subscriber, and a slow
		application subscriber cannot delay a command to a device.
	</p>

