	<h2>What else you get</h2>
	<p>Three things that are not answers to a wall, and one licensing note.</p>

	<h3>Matching cost does not grow with subscriptions</h3>
	<p>
		All active subscriptions live in an in-memory Trie. Lookup time is proportional to the length of the topic, not the
		number of subscriptions — a broker holding a million subscriptions matches a topic as fast as one holding a
		thousand. Adding subscribers does not slow down routing.
	</p>

	<h3>Shared subscriptions that add throughput</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto implements MQTT 5.0 shared subscriptions and so does TBMQ, so this is not a support difference — it is a
		difference in what adding a member does. Register an
		<a href="/docs/application-shared-subscription/">application shared subscription</a> and the group gets its own Kafka
		topic; each subscribing APPLICATION client joins that topic's consumer group, so an extra instance picks up a share of
		the partitions and the group rebalances when one dies. Messages keep accumulating in Kafka while every member is offline,
		and delivery resumes from the committed offset.
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/shared-subscriptions/">Shared subscriptions</a> covers the DEVICE-client behavior and the load-balancing
		strategies.
	</p>

	<h3>The running cluster is inspectable</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto's operational surface is a log file and the <code>$SYS</code> topic tree. TBMQ answers the same questions from
		a <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/monitoring/">web UI</a> over the live cluster: which
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions</a> are connected and what each is subscribed to, per-client and cluster-wide
		metrics, which connection attempts were rejected and why, and a built-in
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/websocket-client/">WebSocket client</a> for publishing a test message without leaving the
		browser. The same objects are reachable over the <a href="/docs/rest-api/">REST API</a>, and metrics export to
		<a href="/docs/prometheus-metrics/">Prometheus</a>.
	</p>

	<h3>Open source, and what Professional Edition adds</h3>
	<p>
		Everything above is Community Edition, <a href="https://github.com/thingsboard/tbmq">Apache 2.0</a>, commercial use
		included. <a href="/pricing/">Professional Edition</a> adds OAuth 2.0 single sign-on, role-based access control, audit
		logs, domain management and white labeling — on the same broker, not a different one.
	</p>

	<h2>What the architecture is built to do</h2>
	<p>Each figure comes from a published test, with its hardware and load profile documented.</p>

	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-split-2" title="3M msg/sec delivered">
			fan-out from a single 32-vCPU node — 1k msg/sec in, 3M out (100 publishers → 3,000 subscribers, QoS 0), 7.4 ms
			average latency, 54% CPU.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:plug-connected" title="100M connections">
			held on a 25-node cluster across 100M unique topics, while moving 6M messages per second at QoS 1.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrows-right-left" title="1M msg/sec point-to-point">
			500,000 publishers to 500,000 persistent device subscribers at QoS 1, on five nodes.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

	<p>
		On methodology: these are TBMQ tests on TBMQ hardware, not a same-hardware comparison.
		<strong>Mosquitto publishes no comparable figures</strong> — the project makes no numeric connection or throughput claim
		at all — so there is no authoritative head-to-head number to quote, from us or anyone else.
	</p>
	<p><a href="/performance/">Full benchmark methodology and results →</a></p>

	<h2>At a glance</h2>
	<div class="overflow-x">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Mosquitto</th><th>TBMQ</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><td>MQTT versions</td><td>3.1, 3.1.1, 5.0</td><td>3.1, 3.1.1, 5.0</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Shared subscriptions</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Clustering</td><td>None — bridging between brokers</td><td>Symmetric multi-node, no coordinator</td></tr
				>
				<tr
					><td>Session &amp; subscription state</td><td>Per broker</td><td>Shared via Kafka, resumable on any node</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr
					><td>Message durability</td><td>Periodic file autosave on the host</td><td>PUBACK only after Kafka stores</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr
					><td>Offline queues</td><td>Per-session message cap, persisted on the same autosave</td><td
						>Per-client Redis/Valkey queue, or dedicated Kafka topic with offset replay</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr
					><td>Authentication</td><td>Password file, TLS, X.509, plugins</td><td
						>Basic, X.509, JWT, HTTP, SCRAM + topic rules</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr
					><td>Management UI</td><td>None — CLI and <code>$SYS</code> topics</td><td
						>Sessions, subscriptions, metrics, WebSocket client</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr><td>REST API</td><td>None</td><td>Sessions, subscriptions, credentials</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Built-in integrations</td><td>None</td><td>HTTP, MQTT, Kafka</td></tr>
				<tr
					><td>Kubernetes deployment</td><td>Official Docker image, community Helm charts</td><td
						>Official Helm charts for EKS, AKS, GKE and minikube</td
					></tr
				>
				<tr><td>Footprint</td><td>Single binary, a few MB</td><td>JVM + Kafka + Redis + PostgreSQL</td></tr>
				<tr><td>License</td><td>EPL-2.0 / EDL-1.0</td><td>Apache-2.0, commercial use included</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>
	<p>
		Every Mosquitto column above describes the open-source Eclipse broker at its defaults. Several of these gaps are
		things Cedalo's commercial Pro Edition fills; none of them are things open-source Mosquitto claims to do.
	</p>

	<h2 id="stay">Where Mosquitto still fits</h2>
	<p>
		Three cases where the move is not worth making yet. If your deployment is a few thousand clients on one host with no
		availability requirement beyond restarting a service, TBMQ's Kafka, Redis and PostgreSQL dependencies cost you
		operational surface and buy you nothing. If the hardware is a gateway or an edge box with a few hundred megabytes of
		RAM, TBMQ is not built for that envelope — though a small broker there can still bridge into a central TBMQ cluster,
		which is the usual shape once there is more than one site. And if you want the protocol and nothing else — no
		cluster, no web UI, no REST API, no integrations — the extra moving parts are a cost rather than a feature.
	</p>

	<h2>Moving over without a flag day</h2>
	<p>
		You do not have to cut over at once, and the two directions come from different sides. TBMQ's MQTT Integration is
		<em>outbound</em> — it bridges TBMQ to an external broker — so the inbound leg uses Mosquitto's own bridge.
	</p>

	<MqttDiagram caption="Both legs run at once, in opposite directions, for as long as the migration takes.">
