			<text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="570" y="115">repointed clients</text>

			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M190 92 C 300 40 380 40 487 92" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path>
			<text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="42">Mosquitto&apos;s own connection bridge</text>

			<path class="d-line" d="M490 112 C 380 164 300 164 193 112" marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path>
			<text class="d-note d-mid" x="340" y="178">TBMQ MQTT Integration (outbound)</text>
		</svg>
	</MqttDiagram>

	<h3>Point Mosquitto's bridge at TBMQ</h3>
	<p>Every existing publisher keeps its current endpoint and its current configuration. Nothing on that side moves.</p>

	<h3>Add TBMQ's MQTT Integration back to Mosquitto</h3>
	<p>
		Legacy consumers keep reading from the broker they already read from, so backend systems need no coordinated change.
	</p>

	<h3>Repoint clients in batches, then drop both bridges</h3>
	<p>Devices first, backend consumers last, and no maintenance window at any point.</p>

	<h3>The parts that are easy to forget</h3>
	<p>Six things that are not in the diagram and are the usual source of surprises.</p>

	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:users-group" title="Set a client type per credential">
			TBMQ routes persistent clients by <a href="/docs/user-guide/mqtt-client-type/">client type</a>, so a backend
			consumer left as DEVICE gets a per-client queue instead of its own Kafka topic. Decide this before you repoint,
			not after.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:key" title="Re-provision credentials">
			A Mosquitto password file and ACL file do not carry over. Create the matching
			<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/mqtt-client-credentials/">client credentials</a> and topic rules in TBMQ first —
			<a href="/docs/other/bulk-provisioning/">bulk provisioning</a> takes a file rather than one form per device.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:route" title="Keep the two bridge legs disjoint">
			Both legs are running at once, so overlapping topic filters will round-trip a message between the brokers. Split
			the filters explicitly and check them before enabling the second leg.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:pin" title="Decide what happens to retained messages">
			The retained set is per-broker state. Plan to re-publish the retained messages that still matter against TBMQ
			rather than assuming a bridge reproduces them.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:list-check" title="Verify each batch before the next">
			After repointing a batch, confirm in TBMQ's <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions view</a> that every client
			is connected with the subscriptions you expect. A silently missing subscription is the failure mode worth catching early.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrow-back-up" title="Keep the rollback until the end">
			Leave both bridges up until the last batch has run clean. Until they come down, reverting a batch is repointing it
			back — no data movement and no restore.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

